  "small": "under 1600 pixels wide: a row or a half-width picture, never full-bleed",
  "prints": "one clear subject on a plain ground: the kind a photo object (assets/objects/photo-*) can print on a software page; never printed on a place page, nor as a hero or a section's main photograph",
  "orientation": "landscape, portrait or square, from ratio (width / height)",
  "tone": "light, mid or dark",
  "quality": "the rating looked at full size (Unsplash 3 to 9, Commons 5 to 9): below 7 (the soft, hazy, foggy and snapshot files) is never dealt and never leads; the q4 fog, haze and cloud files are out of hotel, outdoors and wellness altogether; a lead is 7 or more",
  "people": "people are in it but never as faces: hands, backs, a face out of frame. \"figure\": a whole person stands in it, however small (a surfer, a runner, a figure in fog): never dealt, neither as the lead nor in a section",
  "house": "one house stands alone in it and would pass for the client's own: never dealt to a hotel or guesthouse",
  "weak": "below the set, or a mood frame the skill forbids (a curtain or plant shadows, a sky, fog, an aurora, a waterfall, an animal, a vehicle, a recognisable viewpoint: PH-03). Kept with its credit and its why for the record, no file on disk, never dealt; a whole room (a meeting room, a lounge, a breakfast room) is weak for the same reason a whole room is never dealt",
  "needsSetting": "a frame that reads as one kind of place (a snowy fjord from the air, a snowy ridge in a window): dealt only when --setting names all its terrain, and a land frame only with --own-land and its named place",
  "files": "local WebP files, smallest first: [{ w: real width, file: path under assets/photos }]. fetch copies them (no download): 1200 always, some also 1600, 1800, 2144 or 2400. A file over the cap (hero 400 KB, other 250 KB) is left out of the srcset. The hero's 2x at 1440 (2880) is not in the library",
  "lqip": "a 24 px WebP data URI of the picture: fetch --lqip puts it behind the <img> so a slow connection shows its colours, never an empty frame",
  "craft": "the craft it shows (smith, weaving, printing, wood, instrument, bookbinding): another business's work unless the brief names that craft. A craft in --trade (blacksmith, loom, letterpress, bindery) deals only that craft; no craft named deals none of them (a smithy is never a weaver's bench, a bookshop never shows a bookbinder's hands)",
