# Moments

A moment is the award-site part of a page (house rule 8): the one place, or two, where a page does
something a visitor remembers, in a section of its own; besides it the page has only the hero's
entrance and the hover layer (`motion.md`, The rule). Each is a folder in `assets/moments/<id>/`
(`moment.js`, `moment.css`, `meta.json`, a full `demo.html`); `index.json` lists them,
`gallery.html` links the demos and `moments.js` runs them. Eighteen folders: five retired
(`headline-arrives`, `closing-lift`, `lines-rise`, `window-parallax`, `word-rotate`), and thirteen
dealt by kind (below).

**Which pages: every page.** One moment on a short page, two on a long one (eight sections or more,
in two different sections), never three, on every kind. The place's short page (under 8
photographs, `sections.md` section 4) skips the type moment: its one moment is `picture-develops`
on the section's photograph (`--fits section --photo`, dealt again with `--exclude photo-chapters`
when it gives `photo-chapters`), or, with no photograph, `word-highlight` in a one-screen statement
section of its own, so the page is four screens.

| page kind | dealt from | look |
|---|---|---|
| place (cafe, bakery, guesthouse, shop, gym; a studio with a door is passed as `--page place`, since `--page studio` reads as service) | `photo-chapters`, `picture-develops` (film), `word-highlight` | photographs and type |
| product (site-kind other) | `photo-chapters`, `pinned-chapters`, `picture-develops` (film), `word-highlight` | photographs and type |
| service (firm, clinic, agency, trade) | `photo-chapters`, `pinned-chapters`, `picture-develops` (film), `word-highlight` | photographs and type |
| software, gaming, tech | the eleven that list them: every dealt moment but `photo-chapters` and `picture-develops` | as before 2.5.1 |

Why these for the other kinds: each is led by the client's own photographs or words, moves only in
its own section, costs no WebGL and reads as calm (a hotel, a law firm, a wedding photographer). Not
on them: anything with a shader, WebGL or a liquid or pointer effect (`liquid-hover`,
`liquid-type`, `cursor-spotlight`), anything that reads as a graphic trick (`iris-opens`,
`line-carries-colour`, `letters-assemble`, the halftone of `picture-develops`, and
`giant-type-pass`: a photograph inside letters reads as a template trick, muddies a warm palette
and is a parallax), a loop (`marquee-row`) and a count (`counter-rolls`). On those
pages the two of a long page are the statement moment (`word-highlight`) and one section moment
(the photographs), or the photograph that develops beside one of the chapters moments (two kinds:
one runs once as it enters, the other follows the scroll). `photo-chapters` and `pinned-chapters` never share a page and do different
jobs: photo-chapters tells a story in full-width photographs with one chapter at a time on a panel;
pinned-chapters walks through steps listed beside one frame (how a booking or an order goes).
A moment counts against a tech page's big scene (`effects-usage.md`) as one of its beats.

## Deal them, never choose by taste

```bash
# a place, product or service page: one on a short page, two on eight sections or more
node <skill>/scripts/moments.mjs pick --page place|product|service --count 1|2 --fits section,statement \
  --photo --history .taste-skill/history.json
# a software, gaming or tech page (less a hero moment)
node <skill>/scripts/moments.mjs pick --page <kind> --count 1|2 --fits section,statement \
  [--scene <id>] [--effects <ids>] [--contexts <n>] [--photo] --history .taste-skill/history.json
# a hero moment on tech (0 or 1): the same with --count 1 --fits hero; kept only when the dealt hero lists it
```

- **`--page`** is the page's kind (`SKILL.md` section 1: `software`, `gaming`, `tech`, `place`,
  `product` or `service`; `ai`, `saas`, `technical-product`, `cafe`, `bakery`, `guesthouse`,
  `hotel`, `shop`, `studio`, `agency`, `firm`, `clinic` and a few more are read too). A word that
  names no kind (`other` is three kinds) stops the deal with exit 1: say which.
- **`--photo`** on every place, product or service page (they all have photographs): without it
  the photo moments drop out and a long page cannot be dealt two.
- **`--fits section,statement`:** a statement is a section of its own, so the letters, liquid type,
  word-highlight and the counter come only with it; `section` alone deals none of them.
- **No kind needs `--exclude`:** each pool is what lists the kind in `meta.json` (the short page's
  `photo-chapters`, above, is the one case).
  `picture-develops` is dealt only in its film look, on place, product and service; its halftone
  look stays in the kit as `data-moment-opts='{"look": "print"}'` with its print-shop demo, never
  dealt. A dealt scene's twins and avoidMoments are left out by `--scene <id>` itself.
- **The hero moment** (0 or 1, never on a big-scene hero) is kept only when the dealt hero lists it
  in `heroes.json` (`moments[].id`): that is `liquid-hover` on `split` and
  `photo`, `cursor-spotlight` on `product`, `collage`, `film` and `centred`, `liquid-type` on
  `statement` (tech, a headline of six words or fewer), and none on the others. Off touch only;
  reduced motion rests. Two moments in all at most.
- **The WebGL budget.** A page holds at most 4 live contexts (`perf.md`). The deal adds up what the
  page already spends and never deals a moment whose own `webgl` (in its `meta.json`: only
  `liquid-hover` has 1) would push the total over 4: pass the big scene as `--scene <id>` (its `webgl`
  is read from `assets/scenes/index.json`), each dealt effect in `--effects a,b` (counted as one
  each, the worst case) and anything else in `--contexts <n>` (an object, a component with a canvas).
  The deal prints the total it reached.
- **The rest of the deal.** Placed in different sections. The history's `moments` list is read as
  cycles: once every moment that fits has been dealt, the next cycle starts empty, so an id comes
  back only after all the others and never straight after itself (the three dealt last stay out
  even across a cycle's end). Never two of one kind (hover and liquid count as one: one pointer
  effect a page), never two of one slot, never two that avoid each other, a moment that needs a
  photograph only with `--photo`; on a place, product or service page one statement and one
  section, or two kinds (`picture-develops` with a chapters moment). Write each id, its section and its purpose in the plan (`motion.md`, Why it moves). A dealt moment with
  nothing to carry is dealt again with it added to `--exclude`.
- **Retired** (`meta.retired`, weight 0, never dealt): `headline-arrives` (every hero now enters
  through the motion layer, `motion.md`, The hero's entrance; a moment that also moves the headline
  would be a second entrance) and `closing-lift` (it needs a closing band of its own and its words stand on a
  gradient over the photograph); since 2.5.1 also `lines-rise` (every hero's lines already rise on
  load), `window-parallax` (a parallax on a photograph) and `word-rotate` (the `word` hero carries
  its own live word, `layout-v4.md`). `magnetic-button` (CK-94) is gone.

## Where each fits

| id | kind · fits | phone and reduced motion |
|---|---|---|
| word-rotate | retired · hero, three rounds then rests | the written word stands |
| lines-rise | retired · one section or statement, once | the heading and its lines stand |
| counter-rolls | text · the proof figures, once; never in a card | the figures stand |
| word-highlight | scroll · one statement | runs on a phone; reduced motion: the whole statement in ink |
| giant-type-pass | scroll · one statement with a photograph of mid to dark tones (tech only) | the photograph stands in the letters, smaller type |
| letters-assemble | scroll · one statement of 2-5 words, set very large: its letters fly into the line | the statement as written |
| pinned-chapters | scroll · one feature section of 2-4 steps, two screens live | static rows (or each step with its visual under it) |
| window-parallax | retired · one wide photograph | the photograph stands in its frame |
| marquee-row | scroll · a row of real words (trades, cities, formats; never logos: a logo never moves), with a pause button | the words in a wrapped row |
| line-carries-colour | transition · a section on a new ground keeps it edge to edge; inside, an inset panel in the old ground (even gutter, card corners) is cleared by a line climbing from its foot; each block shows whole once the line has passed it | the section on its own ground, no panel, no line |
| iris-opens | transition · a section on a new ground keeps it edge to edge; inside, an inset panel in the old ground (even gutter, card corners) opens a disc with a hairline accent ring; each block shows whole once the disc holds it | the section on its own ground, no panel, no ring |
| picture-develops | transition · one large photograph with a clear subject, once, 1.6 s (look `film`): it comes up out of a faint grey print, darks first, then colour; the halftone look `print` is not dealt | the film look runs on a phone; reduced motion: the photograph stands |
| photo-chapters | scroll · one section of 3-5 chapters (a title and two lines each, one photograph each) under its h2 and lead: one photograph the width of the page, each next one uncovered from its foot over the last, the chapter on a panel at its lower left; about two thirds of a screen of scroll a chapter, so the section is three screens live (four with four or five chapters); place, product, service | runs on a phone held upright; reduced motion: the chapters side by side, each photograph over its words (a phone stacks them), the section as tall as they are |
| closing-lift | transition · the close, with a photograph | a plain closing section |
| cursor-spotlight | hover · the hero frame | the light rests |
| liquid-type | liquid · a hero or statement headline ripples like water under the pointer, an SVG filter, no WebGL | the headline as written |
| liquid-hover | liquid · one photograph with edges and detail, one WebGL context | the plain photograph, no canvas |

## The still rule (every moment, no exception)

Every moment has a static variant for a **phone** and for **reduced motion**, and a finished state
without script or when its module fails. The loader decides: it hands each moment a `lib` whose
`reduced()` answers `lib.still()`, which is true under `prefers-reduced-motion` and on a phone (under
640px wide, or a touch screen turned sideways under 500px tall). A moment written against `reduced()`
therefore shows its finished state there, and the host carries `data-moment-still="reduced"`,
`"phone"` or `"card"`. **Phones still get a moment:** the ones light enough for a phone (one
picture or one paragraph at a time; transform, clip, filter or colour) say `export const phone =
true` in their `moment.js` and `"phoneRuns": true` in `meta.json`, and run there; only reduced
motion and a card keep them still. Those are `word-highlight`, `picture-develops` (film) and
`photo-chapters` (from 600px of height), which covers every place, product and service deal. The answer is taken when the moment mounts (a window resized across 640px
applies it on the next load; `pinned-chapters` and `photo-chapters` ask again on resize). Each `meta.json` says what the
still shows (`reduced`, `phone`). Hover moments do nothing on touch screens either.

**Nothing on a card moves on scroll** (house rule). A moment sits on a hero, a statement, one
section, a transition or the close, never in a card, a plan, a result or a bento cell: the loader
leaves one that does still (`data-moment-still="card"`, `data-moments-card` on `<html>`) and
`moments.mjs check` calls it a fault.

## Put one on the page

The host is a section of the page (or, for the text moments, the headline or the row inside it);
mark it as the moment's header comment shows (`data-moment="<id>"`, options in `data-moment-opts`).
Every section stays one or two screens tall (`layout-v4.md`, `--h`), live and at rest, but the two
chapters moments: `photo-chapters` is (hold + 1) screens live (three for three chapters, four for
four or five; write no `--h` and no `data-screen` on it: the checker and the layout check both read a
live photo-chapters section as whole screens, four at most) and both are as tall as their content at rest. A transition's
host is the whole section that sits on the new ground, a statement moment is one section with its h2
first, a marquee or counter row merges into the section above as its last row. In `<head>`, the one line plus a `modulepreload` for
the loader, its lib and the dealt moment:

```html
<script>document.documentElement.classList.add('mo-js');setTimeout(()=>document.documentElement.classList.add('mo-safe'),2500)</script>
<link rel="modulepreload" href="assets/moments/moments.js"><link rel="modulepreload" href="assets/moments/lib.js"><link rel="modulepreload" href="assets/moments/<id>/moment.js">
```

Then the loader once. On a page with `system.js`, `system.js` imports it and waits for it. On a page
without, the demo pages' tag at the end of `<body>`:

```html
<script type="module" src="assets/moments/moments.js"></script>
```

The loader links each moment's CSS and starts it; `<html data-moments-ready>` says when. A failed
module gets `data-moment-failed` and the class `mo-safe` on its own element, which the moment's CSS
reads as "show the finished state now". A demo's copy and photographs are placeholders. Every moment
takes the page's tokens (`--bg`, `--ink`, `--muted`, `--accent`, `--line`, the display face).

A moment's section in a page is copied from one of the two page models, which mount moments inside
a starter page and pass the checker: `assets/moments/_demo/in-page.html` (a place:
`photo-chapters`, `word-highlight`) and `in-page-service.html` (a service: `pinned-chapters`,
`picture-develops` in its film look). A moment's own `demo.html` shows it alone and is not a page
model.

## Rules

- **The kind's count, an id once.** `data-moments-over` and `data-moments-repeat` on `<html>` are faults.
- **No random effects as you scroll.** A scroll moment is scrubbed to the scrollbar inside its own
  section, which stays one or two screens tall (`photo-chapters` three or four), and does one purposeful thing; at the section's top
  and bottom it shows a finished state (every transition and `letters-assemble` finish before the
  section's top is a fifth of the screen down), so each judge tile shows one whole section. No card,
  row or tile ever enters on scroll. `consistency.mjs` reports a scroll finding inside the dealt
  `[data-moment]` section; that one is expected, any other is a fault.
- **Content is readable without the motion:** the words are in the HTML, in their finished place; a
  moment never hides a headline on load (the hero's own entrance is the motion layer's, `motion.md`).
- **Photo-led on the other kinds:** a place, product or service page's moment carries the client's
  own photographs (or library ones that pass `photos.md`), each used once on the page, and its
  words are the page's real copy; never a stand-in picture to give the moment something to move.
- **One count a page:** `counter-rolls` or `data-m-count` (`motion.md`), never both.
- **Nothing blinks, bounces or overshoots.** Ease-outs (cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) for a settle),
  600-1,000 ms for a reveal, 1.6-1.8 s for a photograph developing; a scrubbed wipe or change takes
  about half of its chapter's share of the scroll and holds for the rest; a loop runs its rounds and
  rests, or carries a pause button.
- **The page's edges.** A moment's text sits in a `.container` or `.wrap` like every other section: its
  words end on the page's left and right edges. A transition's cover is an inset panel inside the host
  section with an even gutter on every side (card corners, never touching the section's edges), its
  line or ring inside the panel; every heading, paragraph, button and picture appears whole, never cut
  by the line or the disc (each moment's `meta.json`, `look`, says how).
- **Cost:** no WebGL but `liquid-hover` (tech only) (one context, only near the screen; a plain photograph without
  WebGL or when the shader fails). `liquid-type` is one SVG filter on one headline, on only while a
  ripple is alive. Every other moment costs CSS variables, a transform or a clip and a passive
  listener.
- **One pointer effect a page:** a hover or liquid moment rules out `fx-button`, `fx-spotlight` and an
  object that turns to the pointer, and the reverse; `pick` never deals two of them.
- **Photographs:** `giant-type-pass` and `liquid-hover` (tech) want mid to dark tones with edges and detail; a
  pale dusk sky reads lavender in a letter and a flat sea shows no ripple. Never violet or lavender.
- **No captions;** the photograph's credit goes in the footer line.

## Check it

- `node <skill>/scripts/moments.mjs check <page.html> --page <kind> [--scene <id>] [--effects a,b] [--contexts n]`
  reads the built page: how many moments, any twice, retired, off its page kind (a WebGL or effect
  moment on a place is off its kind), in a card, holding a figcaption, two that avoid each other, two
  of one slot on a place, product or service page, the WebGL total against 4, a missing loader. Exit 1
  on a fault.
- `node <skill>/scripts/moments.mjs shoot --out shots/moments --profile <own chrome dir> --port 5720 --only <id> [--reduced]`
  shoots the demo's start, middle and end states at 1440 and 390 in the skill's headless Chrome (WebGL
  on the CPU); the end must be the finished section. Under `--reduced`, and at 390 for a moment
  without `phoneRuns`, it also checks the still rule: the host must say `data-moment-still` and no
  animation may run inside it (a line `STILL?` otherwise). `--probe "<js expression>"` prints a value after each state.
- The headless Chrome draws WebGL on the CPU, so `liquid-hover` shows a fainter ripple there than on a
  GPU and its end state waits 6 s to be the plain picture; a text or scroll moment looks the same on both.
</file>

<file path=".agents/skills/design-taste-frontend/references/motion.md">
# Motion

Every page loads the micro-interaction layer: `<html data-motion>`, `assets/motion/motion.css`
after `ui/cards.css`, `assets/motion/motion.js` (module) after `system.js` (`starter.html` has all
three). **It is not optional:** never remove, skip or override it; if a check complains about it,
fix the markup, not the layer. A missing or invisible hover layer is a fault on every kind (round
C: a fintech, a cafe and a guesthouse page lost motion points because a hovered card or button
showed no change), and since 2.5.1 so is a page without its hero entrance or its dealt moment. Give it markup to act on: a linked card is a `.card`
holding its link (never `.m-nolift`), a photograph that leads somewhere is `<a class="m-zoom">`
round its `<img>` (or `img.frame__img` inside a linked frame), buttons are `.btn`, links in
running text `.link`. Shoot a hovered card and a hovered photograph at 1440 and see
them change: the card 2px up, the photograph at 1.03. `assets/motion/demo.html` shows every interaction. This file
holds every hover, press, loop and reduced-motion number; other files point here.

## The rule: every page moves, in three places

Every page, every kind, has the same three layers of motion, and nothing else moves:

1. **The hero's entrance** on load, once (below): the h1 line by line, then its lead and buttons, the
   picture settling. About 1.1 s, then the hero stands.
2. **One dealt moment** in a section of its own (two on a long page: eight sections or more), big
   enough to remember: `moments.mjs pick --page <kind>` deals it (`moments.md`). On a place, product
   or service page it is photo- or type-led (photographs that change in place, a photograph that
   develops, a statement that lights up); shader and WebGL
   effects stay on tech, gaming and software (house rule 8: a cafe, bakery, guesthouse or shop gets
   photographs, never a shader).
3. **The hover and press layer** below, on every link, button, linked card and linked photograph,
   and the swaps a click or a tap makes (a tab on software, a peek-list's photograph).

On tech, gaming and software pages also: loops inside a card's visual (diagram pulses included),
one timed loop in the hero or the `[data-visual]` frame, one count, and the effect and scene
`effects-usage.md` deals. Other pages have none of those: no ticker, marquee, slideshow, looping
picture, pulsing dot or count. An open-now line may be filled once at load; the panel reads right
without it. A product render is a still by default; live only when asked
(`data-product-mode="live"`), at most one live context, with its pause button in the corner, a still
on a phone and under reduced motion (`effects-objects.md`).

**Scrolling moves nothing but the dealt moment.** No card, row or tile ever fades, rises or staggers
in as the page scrolls (stock reveals), no text moves at another speed than the page (parallax on
text), and the page is never held or steered by script (scroll-jacking, smooth-scroll libraries).
A moment may hold its own section on screen with `position: sticky` while the page scrolls natively.

**Why it moves.** The plan names each motion and its purpose in a few words, one line each: "hero
entrance: the name arrives first, then what to do"; "photo-chapters in Rooms: three rooms shown in
the space of one"; "hover: every link answers". A motion with no purpose is cut. Content is
readable at rest, without script and under reduced motion: motion only ever adds to a page that is
already whole.

## Tokens

`--m-in` 150ms (hover in), `--m-out` 120ms (hover out), `--m-press` 90ms, `--m-panel` 200ms (a menu opening), `--m-close` 120ms, `--m-slide`
240ms (an indicator travelling), `--m-image` 600ms in and `--m-image-out` 400ms, `--m-cycle` 3.2s;
tech loop tokens: `--m-roll` 700ms, `--m-count` 1000ms, `--m-level` .6s, `--m-bob` 3.2s.
Curves: one UI ease-out, `--ease` in `system.css` = cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1) (`--m-ease` and the
fragments' `--u-ease` read it) for every UI step; `--m-ease-enter` cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) for
an entrance settling (the hero, a moment, a digit roll; `--u-spring` in `ui/base.css` is the same
curve); `--m-ease-io` cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1) only for travel; linear for loops and drifts. No
curve passes its mark: no bounce, no overshoot. The motion layer moves transform, scale and opacity
only (a hover also changes colours) and never animates width, height, top, left, margin or padding.
Inside a dealt moment a few more cheap properties move, each named in its `meta.json`: a clip-path
(a wipe), a filter (a photograph developing), the colour of words (a statement lighting up) and,
on tech only, a background position (a photograph passing through type). Distances: card lift 2px, photo zoom 1.02 to 1.03, arrow 3px, menu drop 6px, button step 1px
up (2px on `.btn--xl`; none on the text-link secondary of the `arrow` and `block` looks), press 1px
down and scale .985 (that secondary: scale only); no others (`assets/motion/motion.css`, buttons).

## The hover layer

Hover moves only for a fine pointer (`(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`; keyboard focus shows the
same state anywhere); every hover control also works by tap and keyboard and has a focus state; nothing resizes or pushes a neighbour; no rotate, skew, glow, shine sweep or chip glide.

| Part | Motion | Time |
|---|---|---|
| Button | on hover the fill steps 14% toward the ink (an outline gains a faint fill and a stronger border) and the box steps up 1px (2px on `.btn--xl`); press sinks it to 1px below rest and scales .985, so a hover shot and a press shot each show a move; the text-link secondary of the `arrow` and `block` button looks keeps its underline and does not step (its press scales .985 only); fine pointer only; page CSS never sets a `.btn`'s `transform` or `transition` | 150ms in, 120ms out, 90ms press |
| Arrow | `svg.m-arrow` moves 3px right and settles back; one arrow style a page | 150 / 120ms |
| Link | one underline style at regular weight: running text and `.link` rest on a faint 1px hairline and an ink line draws left to right, leaving to the right; nav and footer words rest bare, darken and draw a 1px line 4px under the word | 150 / 120ms |
| Nav | the words take the drawn underline (no gliding pill on any kind); the panel opens after 150ms of intent, fades in with a 6px drop with its columns together (one surface, never column after column), closes in 120ms; with one open the next word swaps it at once; the chevron turns 180 degrees on tech, flips at once on other | 200ms, 120ms |
| Current page | a 2px bar under its nav word (`aria-current="page"`) | still |
| Tabs (software, click only) | one indicator slides to the clicked tab by transform alone (it takes the new tab's size at once, starts scaled to the old one and settles to scale 1): the ink pill `system.css` draws, its label in the ground colour; the panel swaps (200ms at most); every panel one fixed height; on other no tabs | 240ms |
| Card with a link | lifts 2px, its border darkens a step, an optional soft shadow (blur 24px, 10% ink at most, only on hover) | 180ms in, 120ms out |
| Card without a link | nothing (`.m-nolift`) | |
| Photograph in a linked card or link | scales 1.02 to 1.03 inside its frame (`overflow: hidden`); the frame, card and boxes never grow; never dims, fades or takes an overlay; a product card's name underlines with it (`.m-zoom` for any other wrapper) | 600ms in, 400ms out |
| Focus | a 2px ring in the action colour, 2px off the control, drawn in full in the frame the focus lands; no transition | none |
| Field | the action border and a 3px soft ring | 140ms |
| `.m-switch`, `.m-check` (tech) | the knob slides 16px and stretches 4px while pressed, both by transform; the tick fades in as it grows from 70% to full size; on other they jump | 180, 90, 160ms |
| Copy button (tech) | the glyph swaps to a check in place, holds 1.5 to 2 s, returns | 150ms |

FAQs show every answer open (`system.css`): no accordion, and nothing ever animates height,
max-height or padding. Hooks: `[data-m-loop]` (your own CSS loop, tech), `data-ui-loop` (a card
group's one fragment loop, tech), `data-m-count` (tech hero only), `.m-link`, `.m-zoom`, `.m-nolift`.

## The hero's entrance (every kind)

Once on load the hero arrives, then stands: the h1 rises line by line out of word masks (700ms each,
the lines 80ms apart, at most 240ms between the first and the last; each word starts wholly under
its mask), then whatever follows it in its column (the lead, each button, the facts) fades up 12px,
520ms each, 60ms apart, starting as the last line is half way up; a line above the h1 (a notice, a
pill) comes with its first line. The hero's picture is never hidden and never fades: a photograph
settles from 1.05 to 1 over 1.1 s inside its frame (a full-bleed one inside the hero), a window,
object or collage from .98 over 900ms, each as one block (the words on a menu card or a note in it
never step on their own); a panel the words sit on stands from the first paint. All of it about
1.1 s, ease-out (`--m-ease-enter`), transform, scale and opacity only, once a visit, never again on
scroll.
An h1 that `layout.js` reworks (a live word, letters that answer the pointer, a picture in the
line) rises whole instead of line by line.

The builder writes two things, and nothing else (`starter.html` has both):

```html
<!-- in <head>, after the stylesheets (where starter.html has it): the words wait for motion.js instead of flashing -->
<script>document.documentElement.classList.add('m-enter');setTimeout(()=>document.documentElement.classList.add('m-enter-done'),700)</script>
<!-- on the dealt hero's section -->
<section class="hero hero--split" data-enter aria-labelledby="hero-h">
```

Optional: `data-enter-media` on the hero's picture when it is none of the layouts' own
(`.hero__bg`, `.hero__media`, `.hero__stage`, `.hero__art`, `.hero__window`, `.hero-visual`,
`.ly-collage`); `data-enter-whole` on an h1 that must rise as one; `data-enter="off"` on a hero
that holds a big scene with its own load motion (the checker takes the scene as the entrance, and only
inside such a hero). `motion.js` does the rest (`enter()`): it lends
the h1 its word masks for the length of the entrance and gives the h1 its own nodes back afterwards
(`.is-entering` while it runs, `.is-entered` after). Only the words are hidden, and only while
`html.m-enter` is on and before `motion.js` takes over or the 700ms mark passes: without script,
under reduced motion, in print and with `data-motion="off"` the hero stands as written; when the
script is later than the mark, the words fade in over 400ms and the picture settles by CSS alone. A headless screenshot sees the hero at rest (as it sees a count's final number)
unless `<html data-motion="live">`. Never a loader, a gate, a delay before reading, or a second
entrance (`headline-arrives` is retired for it).

## Loops inside a card's visual (tech)

House rule: a bit faster, and repeating.

- One cycle is 2.5 to 4 s (`--m-cycle`, 3.2 s default), then it starts again. It is seeded on its
  finished picture and never shows an empty window: a ui.js fragment rests 600ms on it before the
  first cycle; a `[data-m-loop]` starts at once; the last 10 to 15% of each cycle holds the finished
  picture, and a reset dims and rewrites, never blanks.
- The pointer or keyboard focus entering its card restarts it from the top (not in its first
  800ms). A paused loop stays paused. The card, its panel and the window never move: no zoom, tilt
  or camera pan inside the window.
- One subject per cycle (a pointer clicks, a status turns, a bar fills, a line is written): one
  change every 1 to 2 s, each change 300ms or less. Several subjects: the next one each cycle. A
  changing label never shows two words at once.
- One fragment loop per card group (`data-ui-loop`); the other fragments hold their finished
  picture and play while the pointer rests on their card. A small diagram card's pulse is that
  card's own loop, on the same cycle, restart and pause rules (house rule: pulses running along the
  lines); system.js never holds it (`data-ui-still` does not reach a diagram). Below the hero, two
  loops are never on one screen: in a group with a pulsing diagram, give the fragments
  `data-ui-still` too, or hold the diagram with `data-dg-still` (it then plays under the pointer).
- A page's own CSS loop sits in `[data-m-loop]`, every keyframe on `var(--m-cycle)`, `--m-still`
  (0 to 1) the finished frame, a 48px band (`--m-safe`) kept clear for the pause button.
- Life signs inside the looping visual only: spinner 1s (12 to 16px, then a check), live dot 2.4s
  (the dot still, a ring grows and fades), caret 1s with a hard step inside a field, level bars .6s.
  No typing dots, no lone pulsing dot elsewhere, no block cursor after a headline.
- Kinds of card story (each in a box of fixed height): a prompt types at 12 to 20 characters a
  second and holds; an answer streams about a bubble a second; a notification enters at the top and
  the oldest leaves through the fade; a checklist ticks about once a second; a calendar's events
  fade in one by one; a pointer glides about 200px in 1 s, presses (scale .96 at least, a 28px ring)
  and holds. A speaker or avatar front item changes no more than every 3 s. A chart stays still.
- Not yet placed, so not used: a stepping slot strip, a before/after divider, path-following items,
  a dock tab that grows, a pointer-linked mascot, a comet along a hero strand.

## Tech budget

- **One timed loop** a page, in the hero or the `[data-visual]` frame, in whatever form (a story, a
  reel, a lit border, a live ground, a turning object, a marquee); its life signs are part of it.
  One timed loop plus the one effect at most; with a live effect in the hero, no other timed loop
  runs. Never two self-moving things in one 900px window, never two loops in one panel. Card loops
  (one per card group), diagram pulses (one per diagram card) and the dealt moments are outside
  this count.
- **A count** once a page (`data-m-count` or the `counter-rolls` moment, never both): on load in the
  hero (1000ms, 1.2 s at most), or as the dealt counter moment in its own section. On the open
  ground, never in a card, panel or `[data-visual]`; its width reserved; the final figure in the
  HTML, shown under reduced motion, without script and on phones; never a year, price or phone
  number. A live counter changes only its last one to three digits.
- **Load:** the hero's entrance above, on every kind. On tech also at most one load-once motion
  inside the `[data-visual]` frame (a visual assembling, 600 to 1200ms), settled by 1.5 s; a click
  swap 400 to 600ms. No gate, loader or intro.
- **Pause:** anything still moving 5 s after it is seen has one round pause button per panel: a
  solid 32px disc, 12px in from the bottom-right (in a card beside its title), always shown or
  fading in on hover and focus, the same size and place on every card, never over copy, never on a
  still. A click freezes the frame and swaps to a play glyph (`ui.pausable(panel, ctrl)` for your
  own movers). Scroll-driven moments need none.
- **Timed loops start on load** and never start, stop or resume on scroll; an IntersectionObserver
  only mounts lazily. A canvas may skip drawing off screen only on a clock from load, so no visible
  start shows on return. A hidden tab may pause everything.

## Scroll

- **Outside the dealt moment's section (and the tech page's big scene), on every kind:** nothing
  animates on scroll. No card, row or tile reveal of any kind (fade, rise, stagger, mask wipe), no
  parallax or drift, no scroll-snap, scroll-tinted ground, progress line, scroll-spy marker or count
  on view.
- **Every kind:** the one or two dealt moments may follow the scroll, each inside its own section
  (`data-moment`; on tech also the big scene, `data-big-scene` with `{"hold": 1}`), which stays 1H
  or 2H (`scenes.md`, Its height; `photo-chapters` is 3H or 4H live, about two thirds of a screen a
  chapter, `moments.md`), pinned only with `position: sticky` inside that box. A scroll
  moment is scrubbed to the scrollbar (it runs back on the way up) and shows a finished state at both
  ends; an enter moment (`picture-develops`) runs once, 1.6 to 1.8 s. Its text is live and visible without the motion (opacity 1,
  4.5:1 at every step); reduced motion and no script show the finished still; phones run the light
  ones (`word-highlight`, the film develop, `photo-chapters`: transform, clip, filter or colour, no
  WebGL) and get the still of the rest; `perf.mjs` passes. It never moves a card, a
  photograph, pricing, an FAQ, a quote block or the footer. Driven by native `animation-timeline`, a
  passive scroll read written to a custom property once a frame, or GSAP ScrollTrigger scoped to that
  section without its pin (tech only). No smooth-scroll library (Lenis, Locomotive, ScrollSmoother)
  on any page, no animation library at all on other: the kit's moments need none.
- The header and the phone bar look the same at every scroll position; the bar only comes up once
  the hero's button has scrolled away and steps down while the screen shows one of its actions
  (system.js, 240ms, at once under reduced motion).

## Reduced motion, no script, phones

Colours still change; menus fade; a press shows as a deeper tone instead of a move; the tab
indicator and the switch knob jump to their place; the tick appears at full size; the underline
appears without travelling (`motion.css`, reduced motion: `transition-property` limited to
opacity and colours on links, `.m-tab-ind`, `.m-switch::before`, `.m-check::before`). The hero
stands as written. Every loop, effect, moment and scene shows its finished frame, marquees stand as a still row, numbers show their final text, pause buttons are hidden. On a
phone card loops and effects hold their finished picture or poster, heavy canvases become stills,
there are no hover states and no pointer simulations; the hero still enters, and the moments light
enough for a phone run there (`moments.md`, The still rule), so a phone page moves in two places too.

## Never

- A card, row or tile that reveals, staggers, slides or fades in as the page scrolls; parallax on
  text; a scroll scrub outside the dealt moment's section (and the tech page's scene); a shader or
  WebGL moment on a place, product or service page.
- A section heading that rises on every section, a second hero entrance, an entrance longer than
  about 1.1 s, or an entrance that hides text a visitor could already be reading.
- A card that tilts, flips, scales, follows the pointer or glows at the cursor; a magnetic button.
- A carousel, a slider that advances by itself, timed tabs, a rotating notice, a logo marquee.
- Blinking at random: twinkling dots, glitter, flicker, hue cycling, scrambled text; every change
  shows a state, a step or real data.
- A loop that plays once (outside a card story's replay), a card loop's cycle over 4 s, two loops in
  one panel.
- A hover slower than its token, an overshooting ease, a delay before a button reacts, a focus ring
  that animates, a second underline or indicator style, an animated width, height or margin.
</file>

<file path=".agents/skills/design-taste-frontend/references/perf.md">
# Performance budget

Run `node scripts/perf.mjs <site/index.html>` (Node 22 or later) on every finished page. It loads the page in headless CPU Chrome at 1440 and 390 wide, waits until the effects near the viewport are mounted, scrolls to the bottom and prints PASS, WARN or FAIL. Fix every FAIL. `--json <file>` also writes the result to that file; `--runs N` sets the number of timing loads (default 3).

Reading the numbers:

- **Timing is a median of 3 loads** with the spread printed (`runs 3, 33..161`). One load on a CPU is noise; a wide spread means the host was busy.
- **Host load** is sampled before and after. Above 50% the check says so and caps timing at WARN: rerun when the machine is idle before believing a timing FAIL.
- **FCP and LCP are CPU-rendered.** Compare pages with each other, never with field data.
- **WebGL off is a proxy, not a verdict.** SwiftShader runs shaders on the CPU and would swamp the main thread, so long tasks and blocking time come from loads where every WebGL piece shows its still fallback. That is different work from the shader: shader compile and link on a real GPU is not measured, and the INFO line with WebGL on is for orientation only.
- Counts (images, contexts, modules) and bytes are exact; they do not depend on host load.

| Check | WARN | FAIL |
|---|---|---|
| Transfer (text gzipped) | above 1500 KiB | above 3000 KiB |
| Images total / largest single image | above 1200 / 350 KiB | above 2400 / 700 KiB |
| JS parsed / CSS | above 450 / 120 KiB | above 900 / 250 KiB |
| Requests | above 60 | above 110 |
| Font families / files / bytes | above 2 / 4 / 160 KiB | above 3 / 6 / 320 KiB |
| `<img>` without width and height (or CSS aspect-ratio) | 1 to 3 | 4 or more |
| `<img>` below the fold and not lazy | 1 to 3 | 4 or more |
| Oversized images, `<img>`, CSS background and SVG (natural over 2.2x the shown width) | 1 to 3 | 4 or more |
| Photos as CSS or SVG background below the fold (cannot be lazy) | 1 to 3 | 4 or more |
| WebGL contexts live (load, bottom, peak) | | above 4 (hard cap) |
| WebGL contexts made over the whole scroll | above 12 | above 24 |
| Long tasks / blocking time, median, WebGL off | above 8 / 1.5 s | above 20 / 4 s |
| FCP / LCP, median | above 2.5 / 4.5 s | above 4.5 / 8 s |
| Layout shift, median | above 0.1 | above 0.25 |
| Effect, object or component module loaded, never mounted | 1 or 2 | 3 or more |
| Broken request, sideways overflow | | any |

Sizes are KiB (1024 bytes). The 2.2x rule already covers a 2x screen; a photo that is also upscaled (natural under 0.75x shown) warns. "Never mounted" counts entry files only (`effect.js`, `object.js`, `component.js`, a ui `fragment.js`); shared helpers such as `photo-kit` never count. An object has no marker, so objects are judged by count: more object modules loaded than canvases or fallback hosts drawn.

## Every visual renders

A visual that shows only its ground (a blank canvas, a photograph never loaded, a reveal that never fired, a picture caught half drawn) is a fault at every width (house rule 2): every picture slot has a real `src`, `width` and `height`; `consistency.mjs` `empty-visual` and `gl-fallback` find the rest, system.js outlines a failed visual in red, `shoot.mjs` names pictures still loading.

## Usual fixes

- **WebGL cost (tech only; 0 contexts on kind other).** At most 4 live contexts per page (house rule): the one effect or big scene plus a dealt moment's own canvas, each showing a poster first, mounted at load or lazily as its section nears the screen (an IntersectionObserver only mounts, it never starts or stops visible motion: `motion.md`). One sample per CSS px or less (0.75 when large), about 1.5 megapixels, pixel ratio 1.5 at most; noise, particles and ray marching at half resolution and 30fps or less; drop the pixel ratio when frames take over 21 to 24ms. Every WebGL piece keeps its still when its shader fails (`effects-usage.md`). After any shader change, `lint-glsl.mjs` in `assets/objects/_print-core` and `_scenes` must exit 0.
- **Draw still grounds once.** A ground that does not move is drawn one frame, kept as a picture, its context released.
- **Fonts.** At most four font files (`layout-v4.md` section 5; perf.mjs warns above four): preload the regular and the display weight, `font-display: swap`, request only used weights.
- **Size images.** AVIF or WebP through `srcset` at 2x the shown width, never more (a full-bleed hero 2880w at 1440, 780w at 390), the hero under 400 KiB and every other photograph under 250 KiB (the check warns at 350 for one image), the page's images under 2,400 KiB at 1440 (the table's FAIL; 1,200 warns), `width` and `height` (or an `aspect-ratio`) on every one and a `--g-alt` fill while it loads. Lazy and `decoding="async"` below the first screen, never lazy in it; the hero eager with `fetchpriority="high"`. A photo below the fold is an `<img>`, not a CSS background. A place with 8 to 20 photographs stays fast this way.
- **No shift (CLS under 0.1).** Reserve space for images, nav and counters. Animate transform and opacity (colours too); `background-position` only on small text spans; never height, padding or layout. Import only modules the page mounts.
- **Scripts.** Deferred modules, no scroll listeners (the tech moments read the scroll as `motion.md` says), no layout reads in move handlers. No entry gate or loader; kind other loads no animation library and no WebGL; a real-time 3D model stays near 2 MB compressed with a poster; video ships separate phone and desktop files.
- **Ready flag.** A page may set `document.documentElement.dataset.ready = '1'` when its first screen is built; the check then stops waiting for effects to mount.
</file>

